# Backend and Wagtail development

- Organize code into cohesive Django apps within `{{ source_directory }}` and
  follow existing imports and app configuration. Keep settings in
  `{{ settings_module }}` and preserve the development/production split.
- Use conventional Django models, forms, views, and querysets. Keep views and
  request handlers focused; extract reusable or complex business operations into
  focused modules when needed. Do not impose a service layer on simple CRUD.
- Put validation where all relevant entry points can use it. Do not assume
  `save()` automatically runs model validation. Use database constraints for
  invariants that must survive concurrent writes and transactions for related
  writes that must succeed together. Avoid hidden side effects in signals.
- Use Wagtail's page, snippet, StreamField, and admin extension APIs rather than
  bypassing its content lifecycle. Consider editor panels, help text, validation,
  allowed parent/child pages, and template rendering when adding content types.
- Preserve revisions, drafts, previews, publishing, scheduling, workflows,
  permissions, and localization where the feature uses them. Public queries must
  respect publication and access restrictions; do not expose drafts or private
  content through custom views or APIs.
- Treat StreamField block names and stored values as persisted content contracts.
  Plan content migrations for renames, removals, or structural changes. Test
  rendering of both existing and newly created content.
- Keep business logic and database queries out of templates. Use Django escaping
  and Wagtail's supported rich-text and image rendering tools; do not mark
  untrusted content safe. Preserve semantic HTML, labels, keyboard access, and
  appropriate alternative text when changing templates or editor interfaces.

# Database changes

- Create and include Django migrations whenever model state changes. Inspect
  generated operations and check that no migrations are missing.
- Consider existing rows before adding required fields, uniqueness constraints,
  or changing types. Split schema and data changes when needed to preserve data.
- Use historical models from the migration app registry in data migrations.
  Consider reversibility and document intentionally irreversible operations.
  Do not rewrite migrations already shared or applied elsewhere.
- Verify relevant migrations against the selected database and test data
  transformations. Avoid relying on SQLite-only behaviour for another database.
{% if database == "sqlite3" %}- The local SQLite database is `db.sqlite3`; do not commit it.
{% else %}- The project uses `{{ database }}`. Preserve its Compose configuration and
  database-specific settings when changing the backend.
{% endif %}- Stop containers with `docker compose down`. Do not delete database files,
  drop databases, flush data, or run `docker compose down --volumes` without
  explicit user authorization for that data reset. Never use production data
  or production credentials for development tests.

# Security and performance

- Keep secrets, `.env`, database files, and uploaded media out of version control.
  Read deployment values from the environment; preserve secure production
  settings and do not disable CSRF, authentication, or authorization to fix tests.
- Enforce permissions server-side for custom views, APIs, admin actions, and
  object access. Validate input and uploads, use ORM parameterization, and avoid
  logging credentials or sensitive content.
- Consider query counts for page listings, StreamField rendering, and related
  objects. Use `select_related`/`prefetch_related` where appropriate, paginate
  unbounded lists, and add query-count tests for relevant performance fixes.
- Introduce caching only with a clear invalidation strategy that respects
  publication state, permissions, and localization where applicable.
